    "q-167615": {
      "dom_id": "question-167615",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266225/assets/c3d286778e489326007b655da6a6a168c287a7918f497ef743d7365eaa001d86.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            If the graph above is generated by the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx),$ then $B=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
      "worked_solution": "To find the value of $B,$ we first need to find the period of the function.\n\nThe graph shows that the function makes $4$ complete cycles on the interval $[0,2\\pi ].$ So, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266225/assets/3507eac70c6af174bd0c561054b20af697e5322082769362999c8ab79e10bac2.png)\n\n  \n\nWe know that for the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx),$ the horizontal scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{B},$ and the period is multiplied by the scaling factor:\n\n$$\n\\text{period}=2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}\n$$\n\nFor our function, the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{B} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{B} \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
