    "q-167619": {
      "dom_id": "question-167619",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find $m,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=0.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $m=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We have $k(x)=2{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}+4.$ \n\nThe slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is equal to ${k}^{′}(0).$ From step 6, the derivative is\n\n$$\n{k}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{x}-4x.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=0$ and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{′}(0) & =2{e}^{0}-4(0) \\\\  & =2(1)+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
