    "q-167620": {
      "dom_id": "question-167620",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The tangent to the curve $y=k(x)$ at the point where $x=0$ intersects the $x$-axis at $(c,0).$ Find the value of $c.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We have $k(x)=2{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}+4.$ In step 7, we found that the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is $m=2.$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate of the curve at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=k(0)=2{e}^{0}-2(0)^{2}+4=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(6) & =2(x-0) \\\\ y-6 & =2x \\\\ y & =2x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $x$-intercept of this tangent line, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2x+6 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-3,0),$ and we have $c=-3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
